ut are therefore one net.\", \"expected\": \"The GPS TIMEPULSE output should be a separate signal net that feeds only the PPS input (ADC_FCC1 / a GPIO) and is not connected to the 3.3 V supply rail. Only the rail decoupling and pull-ups should sit on the supply net.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U10\\\") (pin \\\"4\\\") (pinfunction \\\"TIMEPULSE\\\") (pintype \\\"output\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"C16\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"spice\", \"quote\": \"C16 +3V3 0 100n\"}], \"impact\": \"The GPS module's push-pull PPS output drives directly into the 3.3 V rail, which fights the LDO and the MCU and pull-up network. The rail can be pulled low or damaged, and the PPS signal is never delivered to its intended input.\", \"confidence\": 0.6}, {\"category\": \"wrong_value\", \"components\": [\"U2\"], \"pins\": [\"U2.1\"], \"nets\": [\"+3.3V\"], \"observed\": \"The PCA9685 A0 address pin (U2 pin 1) is tied to the +3.3V net, while A1 through A5 are on GND.\", \"expected\": \"The specification states the PCA9685 I2C address is 0x40, which requires A0 to be tied low (to GND) with A1 to A5 low. Tying A0 high gives 0x41.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U2\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") (pinfunction \\\"A0\\\") (pintype \\\"input\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"spec\", \"quote\": \"NXP PCA9685PW (I2C 16-ch PWM), I2C addr 0x40\"}], \"impact\": \"The FCC will not reach the PWM driver at the expected address 0x40, so servo outputs will not be commanded unless firmware uses 0x41.\", \"confidence\": 0.35}], \"summary\": \"The netlist shows the FCC UART3 signals wired to VCAP regulator pins and the GPS TIMEPULSE output merged into the 3.3 V supply net, both clear electrical faults. A weaker concern is the PCA9685 A0 pin tied high, which conflicts with the specified 0x40 address.\"}",
